xmodel/exact-coframe-background-review-grok-20260824.md:83:### 1.2 Chain rule for tame plane automorphisms — CONFIRMED
xmodel/exact-coframe-background-review-grok-20260824.md:85:Jung (1942, over `C`) and van der Kulk (1953, any field) generate `Aut(C[x,y])` by affine automorphisms and elementary shears (the amalgam `Aff_2 *_{B} J_2`).
xmodel/exact-coframe-background-review-grok-20260824.md:100:To handle affine factors with determinant not equal to 1, induct on the stronger statement: every plane automorphism `H` has `JH = C_H E_H` with `C_H ∈ GL_2(C)` and `E_H ∈ E_2(S)`. Generators satisfy it. If it holds for `F` and `G`, then
xmodel/exact-coframe-background-review-grok-20260824.md:126:Without determinant normalization the unqualified claim “every automorphism Jacobian is elementary” is false, because `E_2 ⊂ SL_2`. The Keller case `det = 1` is exactly the case needed. This is the content of Drensky–Yu’s one-line remark (Trans. AMS 353 (2001), p. 521) that Jung–van der Kulk plus the chain rule put every automorphism Jacobian in `GE_2(K[x,y])`. For det-1 maps, `GE_2 ∩ SL_2 = E_2`: units of `S` are `C^*`, so diagonal matrices with determinant 1 lie in `SL_2(C) = E_2(C)`.
xmodel/exact-coframe-background-review-grok-20260824.md:130:If `(P,Q)` were a determinant-one automorphism, §1.2–1.3 would force `J(P,Q) ∈ E_2(S)`. Contrapositively, a closed-row matrix in `SL_2(S) \ E_2(S)` integrates to a Keller pair that is not an automorphism. No separately exhibited collision is required for the JC2 contradiction. In characteristic zero, a noninjective polynomial endomorphism is already a nonautomorphism; Ax–Grothendieck / Cynk–Rusek would convert injectivity into automorphy, so a nonautomorphism automatically has a collision, but exhibiting it is an independent check, not a missing lemma.
xmodel/exact-coframe-background-review-grok-20260824.md:132:The converse — `J(P,Q) ∈ E_2 ⇒ (P,Q)` is an automorphism — is **not** part of the reviewed statement. The root card, the adversary (§3.1), and the feasibility report all warn that `M ∈ E_2` is not an automorphy certificate. That warning is **in tension with Wright 1978**, cited as standard by Drensky–Yu 2001: if the Jacobian of an endomorphism lies in `GE_2`, then the endomorphism is an automorphism (“weak Jacobian theorem”). The primary Wright PDF was not retrieved in this session (ScienceDirect blocked; DOI `10.1016/0022-4049(78)90003-8` from the 1978 JPAA pagination). Until that paper is read, the converse remains a **GAP**. The reviewed one-way implication does not use it.
xmodel/exact-coframe-background-review-grok-20260824.md:256:Affine Aut-substitution `C^θ` with `θ(x,y) = (αx+βy+ε, γx+δy+ζ)`: the curl ideal in the six affine coefficients has solutions, but every solution has `αδ-βγ = 0`, so `θ` is not an automorphism. No affine Aut-substitution of `C` is closed.
xmodel/exact-coframe-background-review-grok-20260824.md:293:| Jung 184 (1942) 161–174; van der Kulk, Nieuw Arch. Wisk. 1 (1953) 33–41 | every plane automorphism is tame |
xmodel/exact-coframe-background-review-grok-20260824.md:296:| Wright, J. Pure Appl. Algebra 12 (1978) 235–251, MR501951 | amalgamated structure of `GL_2(k[X_1,…,X_n])` **and the weak Jacobian theorem for two variables**. Secondary statement (Drensky–Yu, Trans. AMS 353 (2001) 519–537, p. 521, extracted from the HKU bitstream index of `https://hub.hku.hk/bitstream/10722/48401/1/63202.pdf` at 2026-08-24T06:40Z; the PDF itself was Cloudflare-blocked): “if the Jacobian matrix of an endomorphism `F` of `K[x,y]` belongs to `GE_2(K[x,y])`, then `F` is an automorphism.” Ivarsson–Kutzschebauch 2012 cite Wright 1978 specifically “for a connection to the Jacobian problem on `C^2`.” **Primary Wright PDF not retrieved (ScienceDirect IP block).** |
